# Parking Lot — ANTH Admin Platform

Aspirational and deferred ideas, captured with enough design context to pick
them up cold. Not commitments, not open threads (those live in the project
instructions) — this is the "someday, deliberately" list. When an item ships,
delete it here and let the repo history remember.

---

## 1. Full portal impersonation ("View as", read-only)

**What:** A super_admin opens any user's portal exactly as that user sees it —
nav, modules, data — with all mutation blocked.

**Why parked:** Platform project touching every module; the task-queue viewer
and workload card cover the near-term need.

**Design decisions already made (2026-09-02):**
- **Read-only is the law, enforced at `dispatch`** — the single gateway is the
  chokepoint. UI hiding (the `_canEdit` pattern, generalized as an
  `App.isViewingAs` flag) is courtesy; the server refusal is the lock.
- **Per-module read allowlists, opt-in rollout.** Each handler declares
  `readOnlyActions`; while impersonating, only declared modules appear in the
  nav. No naming-convention shortcuts — some "reads" secretly write (e.g. the
  cycle auto-match persists what it finds), so every module needs a
  sneaky-write review before it's annotated.
- **Stateless:** the view-as target rides on each dispatch call; the server
  re-verifies the REAL caller is super_admin on every call (never trust the
  parameter). Page refresh exits impersonation — by design.
- **Dual-identity audit:** every logged action records both ("fred, viewing as
  jdoe"), or the audit log lies.
- **Known fidelity limit:** Google-layer identity can't be impersonated
  (`Session`, Gmail, Drive sharing views stay the real login). Mostly moot for
  this sheet-backed platform, but say so in the banner docs.
- **Entry/exit:** "View portal as" button per user in Admin → Users; unmissable
  banner with Exit while active.
- **Phase 1 pilot:** platform plumbing (Code.gs, Index.html/_Scripts.html,
  AdminModule) + personnel module annotated first; other modules annotated in a
  follow-up pass after review.

## 2. Super_admin task-queue viewer ("what's on their dashboard")

**What:** A per-user "open their task queue" affordance in Admin → Users,
built on the same `Tasks.forUser(email, roles)` call the dashboard runs — so
it matches what the person sees by construction (including role-pool tasks).

**Why parked:** Superseded in ambition by impersonation (#1); the personnel
workload card answers the module-specific version today. Worth building as a
cheap standalone if #1 stays parked long — it's one gated server action and
one panel.

## 3. Cross-module "all open tasks" admin view

**What:** One super_admin card (Admin module) listing every open task
platform-wide, across modules. Needs a public `Tasks.allOpen()`-style read
(the private `_allOpen` already exists, just unexported).

**Why parked:** No demonstrated need yet; per-user views (#1/#2) were the real
question when this came up.

## 4. Personnel module Settings tab

**What:** A `settings`-keyed tab for module configuration. The key is free —
the old settings-keyed tab was renamed to `calendar` (deployed 2026-09-02).

**Why parked:** Nothing to configure there yet; the assignment-history card
found a better home on the Cases tab. Revisit when 2–3 genuine settings
accumulate (letters-due default, history retention, etc.). NOTE: before
reusing the `settings` key, clear any orphaned ModuleTabs visibility rows from
the pre-rename era, or they'll silently apply to the new tab.

## 5. Deadline moves in the assignment-history trail

**What:** A third `Kind: 'deadline'` in the AssignmentHistory tab, written by
the task-deadline reconcile, so schedule-driven due-date changes appear in the
history panel alongside assignment and status moves.

**Why parked:** Kept scope tight when building the reconcile (2026-09-02);
deadline moves remain visible via the reconcile toast and the audit log's
proposeDate/setCycleAnchors entries. Easy add: one insert in
`_reconcileDraftTaskDueDates`, one render branch in the history panel.

## 6. Instant reconcile for upstream calendar moves

**What:** Today, a deadline moving IN THE DEPARTMENT CALENDAR shifts computed
schedules immediately but re-dates dashboard tasks only at the next
schedule-touching save in the personnel module (documented limit in
`_reconcileDraftTaskDueDates`). Closing the gap means either a time-driven
trigger running the reconcile nightly, or reconcile-on-read (rejected —
side effects in read paths are against platform patterns).

**Why parked:** The lag is acceptable for a test deployment; a nightly trigger
is the likely shape if it ever isn't.
